```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
    % Unit Circle
    \draw[gray!50] (0,0) circle (1);
    \draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (1.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,1.2) node[above] {$y$};

    % Angle and Terminal Side
    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (30:1) node[above right] {$60^\circ$};
    \draw[dashed] (30:1) -- (30:1 |- 0,0) node[below] {$\frac{1}{2}$};

    % Coordinates
    \filldraw (30:1) circle (1pt) node[above right] {$\left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)$};

    % Grid
    \draw[gray!20] (-1,-1) grid (1,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        xmin=-2*pi, xmax=2*pi,
        ymin=-2, ymax=2,
        axis lines=middle,
        grid=both,
        grid style={gray!20},
        xlabel=$x$,
        ylabel=$y$,
        xtick={-2*pi, -3*pi/2, -pi, -pi/2, 0, pi/2, pi, 3*pi/2, 2*pi},
        xticklabels={$-2\pi$, $-\frac{3\pi}{2}$, $-\pi$, $-\frac{\pi}{2}$, $0$, $\frac{\pi}{2}$, $\pi$, $\frac{3\pi}{2}$, $2\pi$},
        ytick={-2, -1, 0, 1, 2},
        yticklabels={$-2$, $-1$, $0$, $1$, $2$},
        samples=100,
        domain=-2*pi:2*pi
    ]
    \addplot[blue, thick] {2*sin(x r)};
    \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
```

[h]Unit Circle Transformation[/h]: The unit circle is fundamental in understanding trigonometric functions. As an angle $\theta$ is drawn from the positive x-axis, the terminal side intersects the unit circle at a point $(x, y)$. This point corresponds to $(\cos(\theta), \sin(\theta))$.

- [u]cosine maps to x[/u]: The x-coordinate of the point on the unit circle is given by $\cos(\theta)$.
- [u]sine maps to y[/u]: The y-coordinate of the point on the unit circle is given by $\sin(\theta)$.

[c]circle[/c]: For $\theta = 60^\circ$ ($\pi/3$ radians), the coordinates are $(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2})$.

The sine wave diagram shows one full period of $f(x) = 2\sin(x)$. The amplitude of the sine function is 2, which means the wave oscillates between $-2$ and $2$. The period of the sine function is $2\pi$, which is the length of one complete cycle of the wave.

[b]box[/b]: The range of $f(x) = 2\sin(x)$ is [b]$[-2, 2]$[/b]. This is because the sine function oscillates between $-1$ and $1$, and multiplying by 2 scales the range to $-2$ and $2$.

